0.01354217529296875, 0.004489898681640625, -0.00601959228515625, 0.01104736328125, 0.0012683868408203125, 0.0105743408203125, 0.05047607421875, 0.035858154296875, 0.0034580230712890625, -0.0022983551025390625, -0.0235748291015625, 0.00514984130859375, 0.03277587890625, -0.0154266357421875, -0.01107025146484375, -0.033294677734375, -0.03179931640625, 0.01273345947265625, -0.0028362274169921875, 0.0084686279296875, -0.0272674560546875, 0.020111083984375, 0.0007266998291015625, 0.00507354736328125, 0.00214385986328125, -0.01474761962890625, -0.00970458984375, 0.00013339519500732422, -0.01088714599609375, 0.01328277587890625, -0.0135955810546875, 0.0219879150390625, 0.0005621910095214844, 0.00650787353515625, 0.00650787353515625, 0.017059326171875, 0.029754638671875, -0.01371002197265625, 0.0352783203125, -0.0222320556640625, -0.0186614990234375, 0.003692626953125, -0.00289154052734375, -0.004627227783203125, 0.002864837646484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. However, this task is rendered complex by the diversity in organizational accounting practices\u2014a phenomenon we describe as \u201cunique accounting contexts.\u201d Given these challenges, our research question is: **Can small, fine-tuned language models (SLMs) for invoice GL code assignment achieve accuracy and interpretability comparable to or better than large, cloud-based models, while remaining suitable for secure on-premise deployment in diverse enterprise settings?** Our main contributions include: (1) systematic identification and integration of client-specific accounting contexts into SLM training; (2) rigorous annotation protocols with high inter-annotator agreement; (3) thorough benchmarking of SLMs versus LLMs and rule-based methods across realistic, multi-client datasets; and (4) comprehensive interpretability and representational analyses to support deployment in regulated environments.\n\n**Unique Accounting Contexts and Data Diversity**\n\nUnique accounting contexts were systematically identified through an initial audit of each organization's chart of accounts, accounting policy documents, and prior GL-coded transaction logs. Criteria for delineation included: (a) custom vendor-to-GL code mappings, (b) presence of custom, nested GL hierarchies, (c) use of nonstandard jargon or acronyms in invoice descriptions, and (d) deviation from accounting industry default categories. For example, while one company categorized software subscriptions as \u201cIT Services,\u201d another used \u201cProfessional Fees\u201d; similarly, invoices from the same vendor could be mapped to different GL codes depending on client-specific usage. These examples illustrate concrete, policy-driven drivers of diversity across organizations.\n\nThis diversity extended to how organizations grouped expenses: some categorized all employee expenses broadly, while others used highly specific GL codes. To capture this, we systematically collected metadata for each client\u2019s chart of accounts and codified explicit GL code assignment rules. These business rules\u2014such as precedence for vendor-specific codes or context-dependent assignment by keyword\u2014were encoded as structured features in our dataset. Our data set spanned invoices collected from companies in three sectors (manufacturing, consulting, logistics), each based in different regions across North America and Europe, covering financial periods from 2019\u20132023 and ranging from monthly to quarterly billing cycles. This diversity enhances the relevance and robustness of our findings.\n\n**Methods**\n\nWe evaluated whether small in-house language models (SLMs)\u2014specifically, SBERT (Sentence-BERT) and DeBERTa (a transformer-based model)\u2014can compete with larger, cloud-based models with respect to accuracy, data security, and interpretability. SLMs here are defined as transformer models small enough for on-premises deployment and feasible client-specific fine-tuning. SBERT was selected for its established sentence-level semantic representations, and DeBERTa for robust contextual encoding particularly in lower resource settings. Hyperparameters (learning rates, optimizer, batch size, epochs) were chosen following recommendations from prior invoice classification literature [1,2] and were tuned via 5-fold cross-validation. We explored grid searches for learning rate (1e-6 to 2e-5), batch sizes (16, 32), and patience values (2\u20135); final choices reflected a balance between maximizing validation F1, minimising overfitting, and practical considerations such as available GPU memory (hardware limited batch size to 32 for most runs) and acceptable training times (<3 hours per model for client deployments). Detailed model configurations and full hyperparameter results are summarized in Supplementary Methods Table 1, accessible at [repository link/Supplementary Information].\n\nOur final dataset comprised 10,000 labeled invoices from mid-sized manufacturing, consulting, and logistics firms, covering 45 GL codes and 300+ vendors, with invoices sampled over four years and various billing cycles. GL code frequencies were highly skewed: the most common label appeared on 3,000 invoices, while the rarest appeared on 30; the top 10 codes comprised 60% of all labels. Preprocessing included lower-casing, tokenization, removal of duplicates, and normalization of numerical fields to strings. For ambiguous or low-confidence field extractions (<0.4 extractor confidence or <5% field occurrence), fields were set to \u201c[BLANK]\u201d (n=422, 4.2% of all entries) or excluded from feature construction. Approximately 7% of invoices contained at least one imputed or omitted field. To mitigate potential bias, invoices with missing fields were confirmed via error analysis (Supplementary Table 3, available at [link]) to have similar code distributions to the overall sample.\n\nAnnotation followed a two-stage process: professional accountants first fine-tuned draft labels generated by accounting software using comprehensive instructions (outlined in Supplementary Protocol 1), emphasizing correct code assignments per each client\u2019s policy and explicit resolution of ambiguous or multi-category items. Ambiguous cases (723 invoices, 7.2%) were independently labeled by two annotators; persistent disagreements (34 cases) were resolved by consensus during adjudication meetings with a senior accountant referencing detailed company-specific policies. Post-consensus Cohen\u2019s kappa was 0.83 on a representative 500-invoice subset, as summarized in Supplementary Table 3.\n\nThe dataset was de-identified in compliance with GDPR and relevant industry privacy requirements. We held out 10% of invoices (1,000), stratified across GL codes, vendors, and time periods, for the final evaluation. Hyperparameter optimization prioritised maximizing mean cross-validation F1 while maintaining training stability; the final configuration (SBERT: 2e-5 learning rate, DeBERTa: 1e-5, batch size 32, patience 3) balanced these concerns.\n\nBaselines comprised: (a) a zero-shot large language model (OpenAI GPT-3 via API) prompted as \u201cAssign the following invoice text to the most appropriate GL code from this list: [GL codes]. Invoice: [invoice text].\u201d We tested five prompt variants, which yielded F1 differences \u22640.02; (b) a vendor history rule, assigning the most frequent GL code per vendor from training data.\n\n**Results**\n\nTable 1 presents core results for the 1,000-invoice test set. SBERT achieved 0.96 accuracy and 0.93 mean micro F1; DeBERTa reached 0.93 accuracy and 0.89 F1. The zero-shot LLM baseline performed at 0.83 accuracy/0.81 F1, and the vendor-based rule achieved 0.74/0.68. Macro-F1 values were 0.84 (SBERT), 0.81 (DeBERTa), 0.69 (LLM), and 0.49 (vendor). For rare GL codes (<1% prevalence), SBERT\u2019s recall averaged 0.39 and precision 0.36, against 0.16/0.13 for the LLM. For the 10 most and least frequent codes, Table 2 details per-class precision, recall, and F1 (full confusion matrix and per-class calibration plots are provided in Supplementary Tables 2 and 4, accessible at [Supplementary Information link]). The lowest per-class F1 for SBERT was 0.37, observed in the smallest and most ambiguous classes.\n\nPractical impacts were substantial: pilot deployment with client finance teams (n=3) showed SLM-based workflows reduced manual review times by ~40% on average and improved code assignment consistency, supporting both cost savings and better compliance. These outcomes were corroborated by pre- and post-implementation process metrics (Supplementary Table 5).\n\nWe assessed robustness using 1,000 stratified bootstrap samples to compute 95% confidence intervals. For example, SBERT accuracy CI was [0.957, 0.965]; differences between SLMs and baselines were statistically significant (p<0.01). Error analysis (Table 3) identified typical confusions, such as \u201coffice supplies\u201d vs. \u201cadmin overhead\u201d and misclassifications for unseen vendors, suggesting areas for future rule refinement. Calibration curves (Supplementary Fig. 1) revealed mild overconfidence for rare codes; overall, SLMs demonstrated moderate calibration (SBERT Brier score 0.13).\n\nSLMs transferred efficiently to new clients: SBERT achieved 0.90 F1 after being fine-tuned on ~100 client invoices. Rare GL codes, though still challenging, saw SLM F1 advantage over baselines of \u22650.15. Class imbalance was addressed using stratified sampling and weighted loss functions; oversampling rare classes yielded \u22640.01 F1 gain but decreased validation stability, justifying our chosen approach.\n\n**Interpretability**\n\nModel interpretability is a core deployment requirement for finance teams and auditors. We visualized SBERT and DeBERTa attention maps (Supplementary Fig. 2) and found that high-confidence predictions consistently emphasized line item descriptions and vendor names; diffuse or inconsistent attention corresponded with low-confidence or incorrect predictions. Quantitatively, integrated gradients analyses ranked line descriptions, vendor, and (where available) item price as the top determinants of model output. In counterfactual testing, altering context-relevant keywords (e.g., \u201crent\u201d to \u201csubscription\u201d) led to systematic GL code changes, supporting the models' semantic reasoning capabilities.\n\nEmbedding space analysis confirmed global anisotropy: invoices clustered tightly by frequent vendor-code pairs. The mean silhouette score was 0.52; intra-class distance for common codes (0.39\u00b10.05) was noticeably lower than for rare codes (0.58\u00b10.09). Notably, frequent vendors had higher per-class F1 and more compact clusters, further demonstrating the link between representation quality and practical classification accuracy.\n\n**Comparisons to Prior Work**\n\nIn Table 4, we directly compare our SLMs to prior results. SBERT outperforms the best result in [1] (SBERT: 0.93 F1 vs. 0.89 for prior domain-tuned"}}